      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — The Fundamental Theorem",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Part 1: \\( \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_a^x f(t)\\,dt=f(x) \\). Part 2: \\( \\displaystyle\\int_a^b f=F(b)-F(a) \\). Use <strong>\\( u \\)-substitution</strong> to reverse the chain rule, and Riemann sums to approximate area.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "title": "Definite Integral and Net Signed Accumulation",
          "xrange": [
            -0.5,
            3.5
          ],
          "yrange": [
            -4,
            2
          ],
          "fns": [
            {
              "fn": "2*x - x^2",
              "color": "var(--fig-indigo-2)",
              "label": "f(x) = 2x - x²"
            }
          ],
          "shade": [
            {
              "fn": "2*x - x^2",
              "x1": 0,
              "x2": 2,
              "color": "var(--fig-indigo-soft)"
            },
            {
              "fn": "2*x - x^2",
              "x1": 2,
              "x2": 3,
              "color": "var(--fig-amber-soft)"
            }
          ],
          "points": [
            {
              "x": 0,
              "y": 0,
              "label": "(0,0)"
            },
            {
              "x": 1,
              "y": 1,
              "label": "Max (1,1)"
            },
            {
              "x": 2,
              "y": 0,
              "label": "Root (2,0)"
            },
            {
              "x": 3,
              "y": -3,
              "label": "(3,-3)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Definite integral \\( \\int_0^3 (2x - x^2)\\,dx = -\\frac{2}{3} \\). The positive area above the \\( x \\)-axis on \\( [0,2] \\) (\\( +\\frac{4}{3} \\)) is cancelled by the negative area below the \\( x \\)-axis on \\( [2,3] \\) (\\( -2 \\)), giving a negative net signed accumulation.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Power rule",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\displaystyle\\int x^{n}dx=\\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "FTC",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\displaystyle\\int_a^b f=F(b)-F(a) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "u-substitution",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\displaystyle\\int f(g)g'\\,dx \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Accumulation",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( \\displaystyle\\int_a^b f \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 6.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Evaluate \\( \\displaystyle\\int_0^2 3x^{2}\\,dx \\).</p>\\[ \\big[x^{3}\\big]_0^2=8. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example — FTC Part 1 &amp; 2",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>If \\(g(x)=\\int_0^x\\sin(t^2)\\,dt\\), find \\(g'(x)\\) and evaluate \\(\\int_1^3(2x-x^2)\\,dx\\).</p><p>FTC Part 1: \\(g'(x)=\\sin(x^2)\\).</p>\\[\\int_1^3(2x-x^2)\\,dx=\\left[x^2-\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_1^3=\\left(9-9\\right)-\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=0-\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠ Common traps — FTC Part 1 chain rule & limits of u-substitution",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>FTC Part 1 with a function in the upper limit:</strong> If \\( g(x) = \\int_a^{u(x)} f(t)\\,dt \\), then by the chain rule: \\( g'(x) = f(u(x)) \\cdot u'(x) \\). A frequent error is omitting the factor \\( u'(x) \\); for example, \\( \\frac{d}{dx}\\int_1^{x^3} \\cos(t)\\,dt = \\cos(x^3) \\cdot 3x^2 \\), NOT \\( \\cos(x^3) \\).</p><p><strong>Changing limits during definite \\( u \\)-substitution:</strong> Always convert the limits of integration from \\( x \\) to \\( u \\) when changing variables: \\( \\int_{x=a}^{x=b} f(g(x))g'(x)\\,dx = \\int_{u=g(a)}^{u=g(b)} f(u)\\,du \\). Writing \\( x \\)-limits on a \\( u \\)-integral is mathematically incorrect and loses notation points.</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0\"><strong>Area vs value of an integral:</strong> A definite integral evaluates <em>net signed area</em> (regions below the \\( x \\)-axis count negatively). If a question asks for total geometric area, you must integrate the absolute value \\( \\int_a^b |f(x)|\\,dx \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Evaluate \\(\\displaystyle\\int\\frac{3x^2}{x^3+1}\\,dx\\).",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "Let \\(u=x^3+1\\), \\(du=3x^2\\,dx\\). Integral \\(=\\int\\dfrac{du}{u}=\\ln|u|+c=\\ln|x^3+1|+c\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Let \\(F(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_2^{x^2} \\sqrt{1 + t^3}\\,dt\\). Find \\(F'(x)\\) using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus and the Chain Rule.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 1 combined with the Chain Rule, if \\(F(x) = \\int_a^{u(x)} f(t)\\,dt\\), then \\(F'(x) = f(u(x)) \\cdot u'(x)\\):</p>\\[ F'(x) = \\sqrt{1 + (x^2)^3} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}[x^2] = \\sqrt{1 + x^6} \\cdot 2x = 2x\\sqrt{1 + x^6}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
